          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus primarily identifies Jonah’s great fish with death, Sheol, or Christ’s tomb and resurrection, while also viewing it as God’s instrument of judgment and rescue. Sea-monsters as hostile rulers or empires are recognized only as a secondary or minority alternative.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies the great fish or sea-monster as a great ruler who personally represents and acts through his kingdom or state, devouring peoples and being compelled to restore what he swallowed.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The great fish is the realm or instrument of death and deliverance that typologically prefigures Christ’s burial and resurrection.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The great fish or sea-monster is a ruler as the personal and representative head of an imperial body.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book elevates ruler-and-empire imagery—Pharaoh/Egypt, Nebuchadnezzar/Babylon, and Caesar/Rome—from a recognized minority application to the governing identification of the symbol. It thereby reclassifies the central Jonah-associated term away from the consensus identification of tomb, death, and providential rescue, while retaining cases already acknowledged under the imperial-monster alternative.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"For the term’s controlling sense, the fish cannot simultaneously be identified primarily as the tomb or death-realm and primarily as the personal head of an empire without allowing distinct contextual senses. The book replaces the consensus’s central referent rather than merely sharpening its boundaries.\",\"rationale\":\"This is not novel because all consensus sets mention sea-monsters as rulers, hostile empires, or powers that swallow Israel; Ezekiel’s Pharaoh and Jeremiah’s Babylon are specifically anticipated. Nevertheless, the book makes that minority strand the core identification and adds a precise ruler-as-corporate-head mechanism, whereas the consensus centers the Jonah/Christ death-and-resurrection typology. The overlap in imperial cases does not preserve the consensus core, so the relationship is divergent rather than refined.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts strongly support Pharaoh and Nebuchadnezzar being portrayed as devouring monsters and Daniel supports interchange between kings and kingdoms, but none directly identifies Jonah’s appointed great fish with such a ruler. John 19 names Caesar but does not supply fish or sea-monster imagery. Thus the citations support the imperial-monster category better than they support applying it as the core meaning of the term “Great Fish.”\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
